WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.220
So, Hank, today marks the 6th anniversary of Vlogbrothers.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.540
But more importantly, last night the United States Senate

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.440
threw the worst New Year's Eve party ever

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.860
that ended with a compromise to avoid the so-called "fiscal cliff"

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.600
of increased taxes and decreased spending.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.780
The centerpiece of this compromise is to make the really hard decisions in a few months.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.180
So... CONGRESS!

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.040
John from the future here. Quick update:

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.760
When making this video I naturally assumed

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.020
that the House of Representatives would also agree to the compromise

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.340
so as to avoid intentionally throwing the nation into a recession

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.080
but it turns out... I may have been overly optimistic.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.260
CONGRESS!

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.860
By almost any measure the last two years have seen

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.180
the least effective congress-ing in the United States

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.540
since at least the 1850's, which as you might remember

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.980
led to a Ken Burns documentary.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.600
Hank, in the last 2 years the U.S. congress has only passed 219 bills,

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.120
which may sound like a lot, except almost all of those

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.920
were to name federal buildings after war heroes.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.500
I mean, don't get me wrong, I am happy that

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.820
there is now a post office named after

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.660
Revolutionary War general Nathaniel Woodhull

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.060
who when ordered to say "God save the Queen" instead said "God save us all" and then got killed,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.800
but that's it, Hank! That's all they've done!

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.200
I mean, the famous "Do-Nothing Congress" of the Harry Truman era

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.040
passed more than 900 bills!

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.840
So, Hank, to understand why the legislative branch

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.420
has become so incredibly ineffective at... legislating,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.760
we need a quick history lesson.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.780
The original American government was founded on the Articles of Confederation

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.020
and that government really couldn't do anything.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.560
So we eventually had to ditch the Articles and write the Constitution,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.140
which created a Congress that could do, you know, just a little bit.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.200
So the United States congress, like a lot of rich people, lives in two houses.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.140
There's the Senate, which has two members from each of the 50 states, who are elected to 6-year terms,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.540
and then there's the House of Representatives, where people serve 2-year terms,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.180
which means they're basically always campaigning.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.660
Each state gets a certain number of representatives based on population,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.580
so, like, Hank, in Montana you just have one because no one lives in Montana,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.900
here in Indiana we have, like, nine.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:41.200
And vitally, each state legislature draws its own lines for where the congressional districts begin and end.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.100
And Hank, I think that turns out to be one of the central reasons why congress can't do anything.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.720
Hank, my congressperson here in Indianapolis is Andre Carson. He's a Democrat.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.160
My across-the-street neighbor, however, is represented by a Republican named Dan Burton.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.480
Is that because we live in different zip codes? No, we live in the same zip code.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.480
It's because the houses across the street are more expensive,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.180
so they re-drew the district to include those houses

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.160
because they figure they'll vote for Republicans.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.120
And Hank, this kind of thing is happening all over the country.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.680
Like, according to analysis by Nate Silver,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.680
in the last 20 years we've gone from 103 swing districts,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.760
that is, districts where either party has about an equal chance of winning,

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.380
to 35 swing districts.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.880
And today, because of the way state legislatures draw these maps,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.740
most congressional districts are so-called "landslide districts",

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.240
which means they're at least 20% more Republican or more Democratic

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.640
than the American population.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.080
Hank, if you're Democrat Andre Carson or Republican Dan Burton

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.300
you're gonna win your general election by, like, 30 percentage points.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.700
So, in most congressional elections, the race between the Republican and the Democrat is not a race...

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.380
the only race, the only thing these people have to fear,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.100
is a primary challenger, another Republican or another Democrat

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.840
coming in and saying "You're not radical enough!"

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.740
So why would you compromise when compromising can cost you your job?

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.640
Hank, 2012 was an amazing year for us...

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.520
you co-created the Lizzie Bennett Diaries and SciShow and Crash Course,

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.120
VidCon doubled in size,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.180
My book "The Fault in Our Stars" was published and so far has spent

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.220
51 consecutive weeks on the New York Times Bestseller List,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.440
why are these things happening?

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.460
Because government! Also Nerdfighteria.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.580
Government, Hank. There's a reason why no popular

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.460
YouTube adaptation of "Pride and Prejudice" has emerged from Somalia...

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.920
they don't have a government. They don't have roads to facilitate commerce,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.100
or public schools to teach kids, or investment in telecom to make... this happen.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.100
So, Hank, congress was designed to suck at doing stuff.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.900
And they do. But they have to work together just enough to cover, you know, the basics.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.580
And we've always gotten there, but now I'm a little worried.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.860
Now, Hank, people won't lose faith in America's ability to govern itself all at once...

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.020
it won't be an event, it'll be a process.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.000
But if it happens, it will be much worse than going over any fiscal cliff.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.380
So remember, the next time you vote the most important election

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.460
might be for, like, state congressperson

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.420
or whoever's gonna draw the line to decide

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.600
whether my neighbor and I should have the same congressional representation

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.240
even though we have different incomes.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.740
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.160
P.S., Hank, who would have thought we'd be doing this for six years?

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.020
Thank you, American government, for running well enough

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.180
that I can make videos in my basement

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.480
for Nerdfighters.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.120
That sounded creepy, but it's not!

